# .openwikiignore — the read boundary for every OpenWiki run in this repo.
#
# Syntax is gitignore-style (comments, blanks, `*`/`**`/`?`, leading `/` to
# anchor at the repo root, trailing `/` for directories, `!` to re-include) with
# LAST-MATCH-WINS ordering. It is a read boundary for OpenWiki's own discovery,
# separate from .gitignore: OpenWiki never reads, scans, or reproduces an
# excluded path, and while any rule is active it also refuses arbitrary shell.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECTION A — the readable slice.  READ THIS BEFORE EDITING.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This file is an ALLOW-LIST: Section A excludes the whole repository with `/*`
# and then re-includes the parts a wiki run may read.
#
# The slice is the RUNNING SYSTEM plus what the repository claims about it —
# backend, analytics engine, UI, contracts, infra, CI, and all of `docs/`. That
# is deliberate and it is a change of kind from the 2026-08-31 bootstrap, which
# re-included `docs/quant/` alone. A documentation-only boundary can find that
# two documents disagree; it cannot find that both are wrong. Three of the seven
# conflicts that run surfaced could not be settled from documentation at all, and
# an eighth defect (a stale `N_eff` formula every doc reproduced *consistently*)
# was invisible to it by construction. The wiki's job now is code↔docs alignment,
# so the code has to be inside the boundary.
#
# `/*` DOES exclude dotted entries — verified against OpenWiki's own matcher, so
# `.git/`, `.claude/`, `.secrets.baseline` and friends are out by default and
# `.github/` is in only because a `!` line below says so.
#
# WHAT IS STILL OUT, on purpose, and why (each is a candidate for a later
# widening, one at a time, with its cost recorded in
# docs/decisions/tooling-adoptions-2026-08.md):
#
#   backend/tests/    ~98k lines — larger than the implementation it tests, and
#                     the marginal claim it buys ("this guard is exercised") is
#                     worth its own measured wave rather than a silent doubling
#                     of the first codebase-wide run.
#   ui/test/          same reasoning, same wave.
#   auth/             the Better Auth sidecar: small, real, and security-
#                     relevant. Next-wave candidate on merit, not on cost.
#   cli/  wallet-setup/  company-site/  nginx/  reports/  skills/  tests/
#                     peripheral surfaces; none is load-bearing for the
#                     alignment question the wiki is being pointed at.
#   submodules/       vendored upstream trees, not hand-authored here.
#   data/             the 14MB corpus manifest and friends. Bulk, not behaviour.
#
# Every one of those has a case in scripts/check_openwiki_ignore.mjs asserting
# it is EXCLUDED today. Widening means moving the case from DENIED to ALLOWED
# and watching the guard go red first — see the header of that script.
#
# ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING. Matching is last-match-wins, so a hard deny placed
# above the allow-list is silently re-included by it — Section B must stay last.
# Verify with `node scripts/check_openwiki_ignore.mjs` after ANY edit here; it
# runs the real OpenWiki matcher against paths that must be rejected, including
# traversal out of the slice and secrets nested inside it.

# Exclude everything at the root, recursively.
/*

# --- the running system ----------------------------------------------------
# backend/ holds the test suite, one-off scripts and a benchmarks doc tree
# beside the package. Re-include the package, the Alembic migrations (the schema
# is the truth `docs/database-architecture.md` is checked against), and the two
# dependency/deploy contracts CLAUDE.md makes claims about — leaving
# `requirements.txt` out while `environment.yml` is in would let a run assert
# alignment having read only one side of it.
!/backend/
/backend/*
!/backend/archimedes/
!/backend/migrations/
!/backend/requirements.txt
!/backend/requirements-base.txt
!/backend/alembic.ini
!/backend/Dockerfile

# The rigor math (pbo.py, purged_kfold.py, walk_forward.py) and the curated
# strategy files the quant docs describe. `artifacts/` and `tests/` stay out.
!/analytics-engine/
/analytics-engine/*
!/analytics-engine/src/
!/analytics-engine/strategies/

# ui/ holds node_modules, build output and the test tree beside the source.
!/ui/
/ui/*
!/ui/src/

!/contracts/
!/infra/
!/scripts/

# CI is where "what blocks a merge" is actually defined; CLAUDE.md's gate table
# is a copy of it, and copies drift.
!/.github/

# --- what the repository claims about itself -------------------------------
!/docs/

# --- root files that are themselves contracts ------------------------------
!/README.md
!/SETUP.md
!/DESIGN.md
!/Makefile
!/mkdocs.yml
!/pytest.ini
!/ruff.toml
!/environment.yml
!/docker-compose.yml
!/docker-compose.local.yml
!/docker-compose.production.yml

# OpenWiki's own output tree and the marker files it maintains must stay
# writable, or the run is denied permission to emit anything.
!/openwiki/
!/AGENTS.md
!/CLAUDE.md

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECTION B — hard denies. These come last so last-match-wins keeps them
# excluded no matter what Section A re-includes later.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- dependencies / vendored code (not hand-authored source) ---
node_modules/
contracts/lib/

# --- build output (regenerated, not a source of truth) ---
ui/dist/
contracts/out/
contracts/cache/
# ABIs are compiler output cached for the backend and UI to read. That they are
# cached there is worth a wiki page; 220KB of JSON is not evidence for it.
contracts/abis/
# Solidity emitted by scripts/gen_asset_universe_doc.py and friends.
contracts/src/generated/

# --- git internals and agent/session-local state (not repo documentation) ---
.git/
.claude/

# --- sibling git worktrees (this repo is worked in named worktrees off the
#     main checkout; none live inside this tree today, but excluded
#     defensively so a future in-tree worktree is never scanned) ---
.git/worktrees/
**/archimedes-wt-*/
**/wt-*/

# --- lockfiles (machine-generated, no narrative content) ---
*.lock
package-lock.json
**/*.lock.hcl

# --- superseded history. `docs/archive/` is superseded BY DEFINITION
#     (docs/CONVENTIONS.md), so a disagreement between an archived doc and the
#     code is not a conflict to report — it is the archive working. Reading it
#     would fill the conflicts page with noise and drown the real findings. ---
docs/archive/

# --- test fixtures over 100KB (bulk sample data, not behavior) ---
backend/tests/fixtures/spy_ohlcv_2004_2026.csv
ui/test/fixtures/**

# --- other bulk/generated data, same cost-bounding rationale as fixtures ---
# 10k-paper corpus manifest (14MB); the corpus is LEXICAL only, no embeddings,
# so reading this file buys tokens without buying signal.
data/corpus/manifest.jsonl
# Timestamped analytics run artifacts and contract-deploy broadcast logs:
# generated output, already .gitignore'd, present in some checkouts.
analytics-engine/artifacts/**
contracts/broadcast/**
# Reference data payloads shipped inside the backend package: a 187KB T-bill
# series, the synthetic universe and its addresses. The code that READS them is
# in the slice; the rows are not behaviour.
backend/archimedes/data/

# --- non-text assets: no claim can be grounded in them, and one of them
#     (ui/src/App.css) is 198KB on its own ---
**/*.css
**/*.svg
**/*.png
**/*.jpg
**/*.jpeg
**/*.gif
**/*.ico
**/*.woff
**/*.woff2
**/*.pkl

# --- secrets, always, in every spelling ---
*.env
.env*
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.p12
**/*.pfx
**/*.crt
# Terraform state and variable files. State can hold real private keys
# (CLAUDE.md § Git safety: the `tls_private_key` resource writes one into
# state), and a widened boundary that reaches infra/ is exactly the boundary
# where a stray local state file would be read. `terraform.tfvars.example` ends
# in `.example`, so it survives these rules and stays readable.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfvars
